  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nStrong low pressure was located over northwestern Ontario early this \nafternoon with low pressure across the Mid-Mississippi Valley. \nBetween these features was a tight pressure gradient across the \nNorthland leading to breezy northeasterly winds, especially across \nwestern Lake Superior. A weak cold front also stretched across lower \nMichigan into Wisconsin with lingering low stratus behind the front. \nAnother area of low stratus was observed in areas along and north of \nthe Iron Range with a swath of clear skies from northern Cass County \ninto northern Aitkin County. The area of high pressure to our north \nwill drift southeast this afternoon into tonight and become centered \njust to the north of Lake Superior. This will lead to diminishing \nwinds into tonight, but areas of low stratus are expected to linger. \nLows tonight will still be able to cool into the middle 30s to lower \n40s and may lead to some patchy frost for areas along and north of \nthe Iron Range.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place for Monday with skies becoming \nmostly sunny. Clear skies and light winds Monday night will allow \nlows to cool into the 30s away from Lake Superior with 40s near the \nlake. Some areas along and north of the Iron Range may cool to \naround freezing, or perhaps just at bit below. A Frost Advisory will \nlikely be needed for most of the Northland for Monday night into \nTuesday morning with the potential for a Freeze Warning for \nKoochiching and northern St. Louis counties. \n\nHeading into Tuesday, the area of high pressure north of Lake \nSuperior will start to move to the east with southeasterly to \nsoutherly flow setting up across the Northland. This will signal the \nstart of a slow warming trend across the region through the \nremainder of the week and into the weekend. Highs Tuesday will be in \nthe 60s with some upper 50s along the North Shore with some onshore \nflow expected. Lows Tuesday night will again be in the 30s away from \nLake Superior and another round of Frost Advisories may be possible.\n\nUpper ridging will build in for the remainder of the week before \nshifting to the east on Saturday. A shortwave will bring some shower \nchances to the area on Thursday, but it will be running into high \npressure to the east and possibly dry air at the low levels. \nAdditional rainfall chances, albeit low, will be seen over the \nweekend. Temperatures will reach into the 60s and lower 70s for \nFriday into the weekend.",
